<Hook>

A flush beats a straight. How many physical five-card hands fall into each category, and what changes when you can choose the best five of seven?

</Hook>

<Idea>

### Give every hand one category

Start with equally likely unordered sets from the full deck. A category must mean the strongest category of the hand. A straight flush is not also counted as an ordinary straight and an ordinary flush. A full house is not counted again as trips or a pair. These exclusions make the categories disjoint, so adding their counts gives the whole sample space.

### Count the strongest categories

For a straight flush, choose one of four suits and one of ten five-rank runs. Those runs go from the ace-low wheel through the ace-high run; ace does not wrap around from king to two. For four of a kind, choose its rank; all four suits are forced, then choose any card of another rank as the kicker.

For a full house, choose the trips rank, three of its suits, a different pair rank, and two of that rank’s suits. The trips and pair ranks have different jobs, so exchanging them makes another hand. For an ordinary flush, choose a suit and five ranks, then remove the ten straight rank sets within that suit.

### Exclude stronger patterns

For an ordinary straight, choose one of the ten rank runs and a suit for each rank. Remove the four assignments in which every card has the same suit. For exactly trips, choose the repeated rank and three suits, two distinct kicker ranks from the other twelve, and a suit for each kicker. Distinct kicker ranks prevent a full house.

For two pair, choose two pair ranks without ordering them, choose two suits for each, then choose a third rank and one suit for the kicker. For one pair, choose its rank and two suits, then three distinct other ranks and one suit for each. Choosing distinct kicker ranks prevents another pair or trips.

For high card, choose five distinct ranks that are not a straight run. For each such rank set, choose five suits but remove the four same-suit assignments. This leaves neither a pair, straight, nor flush. Every factor and subtraction appears in the expandable notebooks below. The category number N in a seven-card notebook names a count, not a probability.

<FrequencyCounts />

### Classify a seven-card set once

Seven cards need a different count. A single seven-card set contains many overlapping five-card subsets. If you count all their categories, you count the same deal repeatedly and can assign several categories to it. Instead list each seven-card set once in increasing card-ID order: first card before second, second before third, and so on. For that set, find the strongest five-card subset and increment only its category’s counter.

Here is the classification mechanism. First test for a straight flush within each suit. Otherwise test four equal ranks, then a trip rank with another pair (a second trip supplies a pair). Otherwise test whether any suit has at least five cards, then any five-rank run, then trips, then at least two pair ranks, then one pair, otherwise high card. Check in that order so a stronger pattern always claims the set first. A seven-card three-pair set goes into two pair, and two trips go into full house.

This is an exact counting algorithm, not a simulation: no set is skipped or repeated. The repository’s exhaustive verifier follows every seven-card set and checks the displayed counters; an independent readable evaluator also checks seeded hands. The live experiment below samples fresh sets and asks whether the selected category matches. Select every five-card and seven-card category to compare the experiment with its exact count.

### Work through one category before opening the whole table

Begin with a full house. It has two ranks with different jobs: one supplies three cards and the other supplies two. Choose the triple's rank, its suits, the pair's different rank, and its suits. Each decision fills a specific part of the final hand. Reversing the jobs changes the hand, so it must not be divided away as a duplicate.

Now compare two pair. The two pair ranks have the same job, so choosing one before the other should not create a second description of the same hand. Choose the rank set without order. This contrast explains why superficially similar category counts can use different factors.

For each remaining category, identify the jobs and then its exclusions. Work through one row until you can reconstruct it; the table is a reference, not a list to memorize in one sitting.

<Shortcut />

