<AtTheTable>

Rankings compare your best five cards against the opponent’s best five. Population counts explain the ranking convention; they do not give your equity on a particular board. For that decision, the known cards and possible opposing hands matter.

</AtTheTable>

<QuantCorner>

Disjoint cases prevent double-counting in risk reports as well as poker. An exhaustive calculation is exact for its stated sample space, while a sample estimates that same quantity with error. Defining the cases correctly matters before choosing either method.

</QuantCorner>
